{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-132974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132974" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132974" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16t^3+24t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132975" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132975" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{6}{125}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{6}{125}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132977" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132977" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=1$ is given by ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12}{2+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132979" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132979" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{66}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{66}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)}=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)=2{t}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $t(2{t}^{2}+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n6-12{t}^{2}=(At+B)t+C(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 6 & =3C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(2)(2(1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A+B+10 \\\\ A+B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(2)(2(-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A-B+10 \\\\ A-B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-16, \\\\ A-B=-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-16$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} & =\\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132981" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cours