                             \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-16$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $u=2{t}^{2}+3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\ln(2t^2+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{66}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-16$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\ln(2t^2+3)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e6bcb0862a8ce9bfeade5187bb0f04f192f46f2bf1b2abcc519f6a6ab8dff16"} {"question" "q-132983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"} {"question" "q-132983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{t^2}{(2t^2+3)^4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062540/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7c2cb045575972c26b75ad06579614f1f153f415946cc57004d57cee7592c5c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2000 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
